  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/5/53/X-Men_Vol_1_4.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The first three pages are filled with Professor X testing first the Beast, then Iceman before pulling out a \"birthday\" cake to celebrate them being together one year.\n\nBut the scene then shifts to <i>another</i> table, where we get our first glimpse of the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, as Mastermind spars with the Toad, then the Scarlet Witch, and finally Quicksilver.&nbsp; Their leader Magneto is elsewhere, securing a freighter from the office of a large shipping line, and it doesn't take him long to gain control over it with the help of his magnetic power.\n\nNot long afterward, the freighter is spotted by a soaring Angel while out on patrol.&nbsp; He takes note of it -- primarily because it is seemingly-<i>abandoned</i> -- and reports its presence back to the Professor, who feels that something is off.&nbsp; An unmanned freighter strikes him as \"ominous.\"\n\nWhen Magneto returns to his \"uncharted island\" headquarters in the Atlantic Ocean with his new destroyer and learns that his assembled team has been bickering, he reminds Quicksilver and the Scarlet Witch what they owe him.&nbsp; It seems they're only serving him to repay some sort of debt (that only Wanda owes him).&nbsp; Magneto reminds her that that he rescued her from a mob of angry villagers in the heart of Europe, who called her a \"scarlet witch\" because of her mutant powers.&nbsp; He also reminds them that they are \"homo superior\" and that they were born to \"rule the Earth!\"&nbsp; Quicksilver isn't sold on it, but as long as his sister chooses to stay with Magneto, he will <i>also</i>, as he has \"no love for humans either.\"\n\nDays later, Xavier reads in the paper about \"one lone freighter attacking an entire South American nation\" (by the name of Santo Marco).&nbsp; He senses that evil mutants must be responsible.&nbsp; He summons his X-Men, but they find him in a trance, mentally in touch with Magneto, who can't understand why Xavier opposes him.&nbsp; The humans must be made <i>slaves</i> and are not worthy to share dominion of the Earth.&nbsp; Very well then -- they will be mortal enemies!&nbsp; So Professor X knows that Magneto is about to launch an all-out offensive against mankind.\n\nSoon, off the coast of Santo Marco, Magneto, with the aid of Mastermind's illusions, are able to fool the city into surrendering, allowing Magneto to form an <i>actual</i> army to serve him, and in short order, he institutes martial law and closes the country's borders.\n\nIt isn't long before a car rolls up to the border, apparently containing \"an American professor and some students\" who arrive on a good-will visit from America.&nbsp; For some reason, Magneto is eager to show such people that \"he is a kind and beloved ruler,\" and they are allowed to enter the country.&nbsp; Magneto can sense Xavier's mental presence and knows that the X-Men are close by!\n\nFrom where they're staying inside the \"imperial palace,\" Professor X now realizes that Magneto isn't the only mutant here, making their entire mission more difficult.&nbsp; Magneto has let them come to him, \"like flies entering a spider's trap,\" and Xavier begins to form a plan of attack.\n\nThe Beast goes out first, but as he creeps into position, the Toad engages him briefly before Mastermind steps in and fools the Beast into thinking he's falling.&nbsp; On the other side of the castle, Angel strikes, but quick as he is, he's not faster than Quicksilver!&nbsp; But the speedster is maneuvered into a wall before the Scarlet Witch appears and sees what Angel has done to her brother.&nbsp; The Scarlet Witch drops the ceiling on Angel, and presto, he becomes their prisoner!\n\nCyclops then arrives and blasts the electric generator, which sends voltage throughout the castle, scattering the evil mutants into another part of the building and finally giving Scott a moment to try and free the Angel.&nbsp; But with no time left to run for it themselves, Cyclops has no choice but to blast the generator right through the side of the building to keep it from harming them.&nbsp; The effort leaves him unconscious, but Iceman soon arrives to free them.\n\nAfter some more confusion, the Beast and Marvel Girl soon join them, and with the help of Professor X, they slip past a particularly nasty illusion cast by Mastermind, but Magneto himself is still at large and now prepares a small bomb to destroy the X-Men -- and a much <i>larger</i> nuclear bomb to wipe the nation of Santo Marco off the face of Earth.&nbsp; Quicksilver can't believe that he would destroy all the innocent people just to facilitate his escape, but it's obvious that Magneto considers the small nation's population completely expendable.\n\nThe X-Men then arrive and avoid being blown to bits, thanks to Professor X hurling himself out of his chair to block Hank from going through the booby-trapped door -- he takes the brunt of its blast.&nbsp; However, the smaller bomb has triggered the larger one's 10-minute countdown leading up to the nuclear blast, and Magneto orders them to flee for their lives.\n\nYet Quicksilver decides he can't let the larger bomb explode and doubles back long enough to defuse it.&nbsp; Though he won't allow the bomb to destroy a nation, he categorically declares to the onlooking X-Men that <i>they</i> are the betrayers of homo superior and that they should \"expect no mercy next time we meet!\"&nbsp; And with that, he races back to rejoin Magneto and the other evil mutants.\n\nThe X-Men gather around their injured leader, who informs them that his mental power has been deadened by the small explosion.&nbsp; Though he orders them to pursue the fleeing evil mutants, they elect to stay with him.&nbsp; But now that he is powerless, they know that, from this point onward, they'll be on their <i>own</i> the next time.&nbsp; They must prove themselves <i>worthy</i> of all the training they've received.\n\nWill it prove to be sufficient?\n[BR] That's the question we're left with as the story concludes.",
  "grade_one_liner": "Magneto conquers a country and gets a nuclear bomb, all in a shaky premise.\nBut along for the ride are allies who feel more interesting than the title characters.",
  "subtitle": "The Best Mutants Aren't the X-Men",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | BirthdayCake | width=11%] The occasion of the school's one-year anniversary was a decent way to start things off, as it clarifies exactly how much time has passed since the title's first issue.\n[BR]\n[BR] (Or at least I <i>think</i> it does -- the Professor isn't clear whether the anniversary is from the occasion of that issue, or from the when he first <i>opened</i> the school.)\n\n[ASSET: right | FourNewMutants | width=13%] Whatever the case, it's presumably been <i>months</i> since [LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25], which certainly has afforded time for Magneto (who made his first appearance in that issue) to have rescued the Scarlet Witch from the angry villagers in Europe and later assemble his Brotherhood of Evil Mutants.&nbsp; (The names of Pietro and Wanda are provided on pages 4 and 5 respectively.)\n\nThough the \"leader\" the four newcomers keep mentioning in the early moments isn't revealed as Magneto until page 6, it can't <i>possibly</i> have come as a surprise to readers, particularly since the cover of the magazine pretty much spells it out -- the Master of Magnetism <b>dominating</b> the image and boldly announcing that the story features the [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=Brotherhood of Evil Mutants].&nbsp; (We can easily assume that we've just been introduced to the <i>lot</i> of them now.)\n\nWhen Magneto first appears, he is \"recognized\" by the shipping line office employee, which is initially surprising since, as far as we know, Magneto has only appeared once before (perhaps even as long ago as an entire <i>year</i> -- again we're not sure), and this only to one military facility back in issue 1 (Cape Citadel), but as he did announce his coming with <i>skywriting</i> (see its page 13), we can easily assume some member of the press saw it, perhaps even took a photo, and announced his presence to the world.&nbsp; (Let's face it:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he wasn't exactly <i>subtle</i>!])\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe newly-introduced characters are quite a mix.\n\n[HANG0] * The Toad is sniveling, but it seems obvious enough that this is to be his role.\n[HANG0] * Mastermind is cruel and enjoys toying with his victims.\n[HANG0] * The siblings are obviously <i>not</i> buying everything that Magneto is selling, but they owe him a debt and so are serving him, at least for the moment.\n\n[ASSET: left | AnUnpaidDebt | width=22%] It is this last pair that prove to be the most interesting, because Magneto himself recounts how he saved Wanda's life from a group of \"superstitious villagers\" in Europe (though I note in the flashback she is called a \"[SPAN: severity=error; text=Scarlet Witch]\" even by <i>them</i>, though there is not a <i>shred</i> of scarlet clothes on her body -- <i>very odd</i>).\n[BR]\n[BR] Be that as it may, the issue suggests it is <i>Wanda</i> who owes Magneto this \"debt\" and that Pietro is only sticking around to be with his sister.&nbsp; (One is forced to wonder where <i>he</i> was when the villagers were attacking his sister, but let's not get <i>too</i> nit-picky...)\n\nThe long and short of it is that <i>neither</i> of them seem happy with Magneto's desire to \"rule the Earth\" but since they also have no love of <i>humans</i>, they're present.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=As a reader, even just this short bit of characterization is enough to elicit <i>some</i> small amount of empathy for the pair.]&nbsp; Not only that, but Stan sells their participation quite well by having Quicksilver add, \"Why <i>should</i> we love the homo sapiens??&nbsp; They hate us -- fear us because of our superior power!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=If the debt alone wasn't motivation <i>enough</i>, the humans' distrust of mutants is <i>additional</i> incentive!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This is textured characterization at its <i>best</i>, and to that, I say] [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=small; fonttype=bold; text=well done!)]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But to artist Jack Kirby, I simply must point out that Wanda's horrible wrap-around tiara is nothing less than a <i>crime against fashion</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | MentalMeeting | width=13%] The inevitable conflict between the X-Men and the new Brotherhood of Evil Mutants really kicks off on page 10 when the Professor senses Magneto's thoughts, and the two mentally argue over the fate of humanity.\n[BR]\n[BR] They [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=clearly disagree]<b> </b>on how it is to be treated.\n\nWhy Magneto chooses the small South American nation of Santo Marco is not entirely clear, but we can perhaps assume that he targeted it as a small test case.&nbsp; His previous attempt to take over Cape Citadel didn't work out (as seen in [LINK:1963:XMEN_1|XMEN #1|2026-11-25]), so now that he has allies, one can only suppose that he's trying to conquer a small nation, away from the United States and using it as some sort of \"proof of concept.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Obviously, this is all conjecture.)]\n\nRegardless, the \"proud people of Santo Marcos\" prove to be no match for the Brotherhood, and they quickly succumb.\n\n[ASSET: right | KindBelovedRuler | width=18%] What makes far <i>less</i> sense is the motivation behind Magneto seeming to <i>care</i> about whether he's a \"kind and beloved ruler\" (which the guard in the panel thinks when the \"American professor and some students\" drive up).&nbsp; It <i>may</i> have simply been some kind of cover story told to this border guard so that he would let the Professor and his students pass.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even in only his second appearance, Magneto's style belies this sentiment.&nbsp; He doesn't strike one as the sort to obsess over how the world perceives him, so [SPAN: severity=warning; text=I find it hard to buy this!]\n[BR]\n[BR] To me, what seems <i>more</i> likely is that this is just an ill-conceived comment randomly thrown in by Stan, and because he serves as both writer <i>and</i> editor, they just slip through the cracks, such as the one in the <i>previous</i> issue, which was a <i>legendary</i> face-plant (see my [LINK:1963:XMEN_3|XMEN #3|2027-03-10] review).\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhat follows is largely Jack Kirby consuming the rest of the issue with battle panels.&nbsp; From page 13 onward, it's pretty much the X-Men versus the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, and while I admit that it was necessary for the title heroes to meet their various opponents, for me, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=it was <i>way</i> too much,] particularly since I found the whole premise of the X-Men being allowed into the country in the <i>first place</i> highly suspect.\n\nSuffice it to say that the introductions are made, the X-Men take note of the abilities displayed by the evil mutants, and nothing much of interest happens until Magneto announces to his followers that he's prepared two bombs to deal with the X-Men once and for all.\n\n[ASSET: center | OverTheTopVillainy | width=large]\n\nFirst off, let's just set aside the question of [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=\"Where did Magneto get hold of a nuclear bomb?\"]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It's a comic book, and I guess the creators needed something for Quicksilver to <i>defuse</i>... is that reason <i>enough</i>?&nbsp; <i>Hmm</i>...)]\n\n<i>Far</i> more importantly, both Quicksilver and the Scarlet Witch seem horrified that their leader is willing to [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=destroy the entire population of Santo Marcos simply to ensure the destruction of the X-Men!]\n\n[ASSET: left | QuicksilverDoublesBack | width=30%] This leads Quicksilver to double back, [SPAN: severity=success; text=which was nice.]\n[BR]\n[BR] But just as nice was Stan's dialogue that makes it clear how Quicksilver views humanity, and that the X-Men seem willing to stick their necks out for mere humans.&nbsp; He may not be buying everything that Magneto is selling, but the issue makes it clear he's no fan of homo sapiens either!\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=(Once again, <i>excellent</i> characterization!)]\n\nThe issue ends with the evil mutants getting away because the X-Men choose to remain with the injured Professor X.\n\nThey're now alone.&nbsp; Will the training they've already received be enough?\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Tune in next issue!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe issue was longer than it needed to be, but that is simply the cost of having to fill 23 pages of material.&nbsp; About three of them were used showing the opening Danger Room sequence, followed later by 9-10 more containing little more than \"the X-Men versus the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants.\"\n\nTaking stock of who these Evil Mutants are:\n[HANG] * We have Magneto, <i>already</i> shown as having no regard for humans, the book's clear super-villain nemesis.\n[HANG] * <b>Toad</b> is nothing more than a sniveling stooge whose only purpose in life seems to be hanging around with more powerful people.\n[HANG] * <b>Mastermind</b> is cruel and enjoys tormenting people.&nbsp; Page 12 even has him complimenting Magneto on his particular \"brand of justice.\"\n\nAnd that leaves only the two siblings:&nbsp; <b>Scarlet Witch and Quicksilver</b>.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=It's probably no accident that they're the only ones deemed important enough to receive even a short backstory.]\n\nTheir motivations for joining the Brotherhood and their internal conflicts about what that membership means are by <i>far</i> the most intriguing part of this entire story.&nbsp; Wanda's participation seems obligatory, and Pietro's participation seems motivated by a desire to stay with his sister.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=For his part, writer Stan Lee leaves no doubt that this isn't the <i>only</i> reason.]\n\nIn fact, as interesting as last month's new characterization of the various X-Men was, it isn't emphasized this time around at all, and as a result, it takes a back seat to the far more <i>fascinating</i> pair introduced here.&nbsp; Say what you want about the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants.&nbsp; To Magneto, it's a logical step forward, as he seems driven to demonstrate that humans don't deserve to be masters of the globe anymore.\n\nVillainy?&nbsp; Tyranny?&nbsp; <i>No doubt about it!</i>  [SPAN: severity=error; text=This guy is pure evil!]\n[BR] But by the same token, his motivation is at least <i>logical</i>, and it makes him an [SPAN: severity=success; text=intriguing character.]\n\nAdd in the <i>wonderfully</i> tormented siblings, the whole notion of a banded-together \"Brotherhood of Evil Mutants,\" and an injured Professor X that threatens to leave the title characters leaderless, and you can almost overlook the fact that nearly half the book was spent in Kirby-inspired action panels.\n\nIn other words, this month's story gets a solid grade more for the introduction of the four new evil mutants than it does for its overall plot, or the continued interest in its title characters (who at times seemed almost incidental to what was going on).\n\nA reader may disagree with Magneto's decision to conquer a small nation.\n[BR] Was it entirely necessary?&nbsp; Was it perhaps a bit rushed?&nbsp; (<i>Who knows?</i>)\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=It was a dastardly scheme,] but that Professor X and his X-Men seemed way too on top of right from the beginning, arriving, it seems, almost before Magneto had even finished establishing himself as ruler.&nbsp; Not only did Magneto seem to welcome them in, he didn't seem well-prepared for their arrival.\n\nAnd then there's that... <i>uh</i>.&nbsp; \"small\" matter of where he got hold of a nuclear bomb.\n[BR] I'm sure they're not just sitting around in the hulls of any freighters, even if he <i>did</i> steal it!\n\nThrow in what seems like utter nonsense about someone like Magneto seeming to care whether or not he's considered a benevolent leader, and you have some significant problems with the story.&nbsp; The fact that he was willing to nuke the entire country makes these sorts of things <i>highly</i> suspect!\n\nI did end up enjoying the issue, despite its various problems, because, when all is said and done, [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=beneath all the punching, plotting, and questionable nuclear procurement lies the debut of a villainous cast that seems <b>far more interesting</b> than the scheme they spend this issue pursuing.]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] Interesting that the Scarlet Witch is drawn with a [SPAN: severity=success; text=green] costume on the cover -- hardly a single thing [SPAN: severity=error; text=scarlet] about her outside her possible hair color.&nbsp; Pietro's outfit is also mis-colored, being blue on the cover, yet green on the inner pages.\n[BR]\n[BR] I can <i>sort</i> of understand the desire not to put Magneto's red uniform next to a scarlet-clad witch on the cover, but one might counter this objection by pointing out that her actual <i>name</i> pretty much <i>declares</i> the color.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=There isn't a lot of wiggle room here!]\n\n[HANG0] The Beast once again has gloves on in the earlier part of the book (e.g. the splash page through around page 4), but they disappear later (starting on around page 13) for the most part before finally re-appearing again on the issue's 4<sup>th</sup>-to-last panel!&nbsp; (Page 22 even shows them on and off again -- <i>in between</i> panels!)&nbsp; Someone is confused... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=having too many characters dressed in the same costumes can lead to these types of mistakes.]\n\n[HANG0] It seems that South American countries were on Stan's mind this month, as [LINK:1964:TTA_54_giant0|TTA #54|2027-05-01] has \"Santo Rico,\" whereas this issue has \"Santo Marco.\"&nbsp; Both of these issues were published on the same day."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "As a follow-up on my announcement in my XMEN #1 review that the name \"Magneto\" is correctly pronounced as \"magnet\" and \"o\" -- page 15 has the Angel proclaim (in a sing-song voice):&nbsp; \"Oh <b>Magneeeeeeto</b> -- where <i>are</i> you??\"&nbsp; This was <i>clearly</i> done in a mocking tone, very much the same way that Ben has called the Sub-Mariner the \"Sub-Mar<b>EEEE</b>ner\" -- making a point to say it wrong... <i>on purpose</i>.",
  "highlight": "Magneto acquires a country, a nuclear bomb, and the Brotherhood of Evil Mutants, while the Scarlet Witch and Quicksilver quietly steal the issue."
}